en:
Well, if you like movies, I think you and I will get along.
Sometime, let's sit down and talk about movies all night long.
Just the two of us, until morning... hm hm.{p}

Nearly all of them wanted to act, and besides, Tokita was the society's founder.
And he was the one who'd brought the equipment, too.
The upperclassmen gladly agreed to let him direct.{p}

"I'm sure the scene I shot was a long shot of the mummy from far away...
But here the mummy's in close-up."
He found himself staring at the film, transfixed.{p}

This time there was no doubt about it: he had never shot a scene like this.
But it was truly well done.
So, without hesitation, he edited that scene in too, and put it in the final cut.{p}

And each time he watched it, a different terror seized him.
This was more convincing acting than he'd seen in any movie.
{g}And the makeup was magnificent.{p}

You would, wouldn't you?
Anyone who heard him talk like that couldn't just sit still.
Not a true movie lover, anyway.{p}

It's a strange feeling, watching a film that isn't finished yet.
It was the first time I'd ever seen one, after all.
It felt very fresh.{p}

Suddenly, for some reason, Tokita cried out.
"What's wrong?"
I had no idea what had happened, and the question slipped out of me before I could think.{p}

And I went straight home.
No matter who I told, nobody would believe a story like this.
I decided I had seen nothing, and heard nothing.{p}

Did I take it home without realizing, or did someone quietly slip it into my pocket?
It was the reel with the scene where Tokita is killed.
Yes, the very same film I saw in the audiovisual room.{p}

But then, how can this be?
When I watched it again a while later, Tokita's murdered body was rotting more and more.
As if time flowed in the world inside that film just as it does in the real world.{p}

So, at last, it's the final person's turn.
...I'm looking forward to it.
Hm hm hm hm hm...{p}

As usual, Tokita was in the classroom after school, excitedly looking over the videotapes he had received that day.
For someone who loves films, just sitting in front of a pile of tapes wondering which one to watch is pure bliss.
Then, when he looked at the last tape he took out, it had no title.{p}

The next day, when he got to school bleary-eyed, a high school girl had been killed.
Torn apart, mercilessly, at that...
Passing by, Tokita instinctively looked away.{p}

It was the same schoolyard scene he had seen yesterday.
Then came night, the area deserted and hushed.
"Now comes the killing."{p}

There stood the Mummy Man.
"Waaaaah!"
His scream echoed through the whole school.{p}

I see.
Then, so that you'll come to like movies, {a}, shall I lend you a good video sometime?
I think movies are a wonderful thing.{p}

All that was left was the editing.
You take the scenes shot separately and splice them together one by one.
Apparently it's really hard work.{p}

This time there was no doubt about it: he had never shot a scene like this.
But it was truly well done.
So, without hesitation, he edited that scene in too, and put it in the final cut.{p}

When he thought that, he wanted to keep editing more than anything else.
And the film he watched that day showed something even stranger.
It was the scene where the hero takes down one assassin after another on his way to defeat the organization's boss.{p}

I started to feel very sick.
I happened to look up.
On the white wall I'd been using as a screen, Tokita's mangled body was still showing...{p}

Just then, Tokita lifted his crushed face and looked at me.
It was so sudden that my legs froze and I couldn't move.
And then Tokita leapt out of the white wall.{p}

Then one day, during work on the school's sewage tank, a strange stone came up out of the ground.
Apparently there were records of a meteorite falling here long ago, so people figured this stone was that meteorite.
So it was decided that it would be investigated at a later date, and the construction was halted.{p}

The old school building didn't have a very good reputation, so Tokita hesitated a little.
But because of that meteorite, his hopes outweighed his unease.
He went into the old school building and peeked into the classroom nearest the entrance.{p}

The creature was there, and it was eating something.
It was crunching away on something like a red stick.
After chewing for a while, the creature spat something out.{p}

And then his blood spread across the ground.
Well, by that point, nothing Tokita could have done would have stopped the suicide.
He thought he had to finish this documentary and show it to the public.{p}

Even so, Tokita kept at it, helped by the very few who shared his ambition, handling everything from odd jobs to acting.
Tokita wrote the scenario too, you know.
The basic plot goes like this.{p}

The Film Research Society didn't have a club room, so it used the audiovisual room after school.
It was still only a society, not an official club, after all.
With practiced hands, he set up the freshly edited film.{p}

And he grabbed Tokita by the neck, of all things.
All I could do was stand there, frozen.
Nakayama pressed his festering face against Tokita's cheek.{p}

I tore my eyes away from him and looked up at the screen.
There stood Nakayama, looking down at Tokita with a sly grin.
I ran from that place in a blind panic.{p}

The next day, he went to the audiovisual room to put that film away for safekeeping.
And there was the film, set up on the editing equipment as if to say, 'Please edit me.'
For a while, he couldn't move from that spot.{p}

The videotape showed a perfectly ordinary view of the school.
"Huh, just a botched home video."
Thinking that, he was about to pull the tape out and go home when it happened.{p}

"Huh? That's me on there...
When was this taken?"
Curious, he kept on watching.{p}

The footage ended there.
(That's weird. I never did anything like this. Maybe it isn't me?)
The date on the footage was tonight, late at night.{p}

Then he waited a while for something to happen.
Suddenly, a lukewarm breeze blew past.
"This is kind of creepy."{p}

I knew it was thoughtless at a time like that, but it was a rather important tape.
They let me into his room, and I searched for it.
My tape didn't have a label on it, so I mostly looked through the tapes without labels.{p}

"Huh, that's Tokita."
It was a video of Tokita meeting a girl at school.
Led by the girl, he headed for the old school building.{p}

When they went into the old school building, the girl suddenly vanished.
The Tokita in the video searched all over the old building.
And then, when he reached a classroom on the second floor...{p}

The girl was standing there.
She was holding a huge scythe.
And the scythe came swinging down onto his neck.{p}

The next day, he went to the audiovisual room to put that film away for safekeeping.
And there was the film, set up on the editing equipment as if to say, 'Please edit me.'
For a while, he couldn't move from that spot.{p}

But when I looked closely, it was different.
I was wrong to think no one was there.
Right beside the spot where Tokita's body had been, someone was standing there vacantly.{p}

After a while, Arai came back.
Maybe it was just me, but he looked pale.
"Arai, are you all right?"{p}
